and clearly not be  that great at it  like he's fine but his ratio of wins to  losses is so far in the red it looks  like his bandana  meanwhile leo's like the easy-going  chill guy that sort of along for the  ride and when the time comes that raft's  leadership  fails leo steps up from the background  and shows how it's really done  he doesn't aspire to be the leader at  all he never challenges raph's position  and  just hangs out but we see many glimpses  into the fact that he's just naturally  the best choice for the leader  and i dig how quietly they build this up  over both seasons  oh then blue you are the leader now wait  what  splinter is probably the farthest  departure from his usual self  every version of splinters so far is  pretty much the same personality wise  but this guy's got some subtle new  complexity  initially he seems like a grouchy sack  of rat that just lays around all day and  makes fun of the turtles  hey pop any chance we could have the  living room tonight  and i thought purple was the funny one i  told you guys i was the funniest  pretty lame and i get why the reaction  was so volatile  but it turns out he's got more going on  as you watch the series  he's trained them through a mr miyagi  method of doing it without them  knowing it the turtles spend all day  watching and replicating fight moves  from an old jackie chan style martial  arts actor named  lujitsu and splinter has trained them to  idolize this guy in a way that they  don't for him  they see splinter as their grouchy  father but they see lujitsu as a hero  and an inspiration for mastering the  ways of the ninja  he makes them watch movies of lou  fighting guys with dead fish and step  ladders and they start to think it's  never going to be relevant  until the day they find themselves with  no weapons except  and you get the rest his teaching is  subtle and indirect but he still gave  them the tools to survive some pretty  crazy circumstances  beyond that splinter's greatest secret  is that he's not only the mutated hamato  yoshi  he is lujitsu the martial arts master  and movie star  after being mutated his body let itself  go somewhat but his skills as a fighter  never  faded that's why it's so cool to finally  see him get out of the chair and teach  the boys a lesson when they  underestimate him in a fight  he's just as badass as ever upon  learning his human identity the kids all  discover a newfound respect for their  father  and start following his guidance more as  far as we're concerned we live with our  hero  tell us about that stunt man again oh  chad what a prankster  and as a result of that respect he's not  as afraid to teach them more directly  about responsibility  and reaches out to them emotionally we  see him becoming more active  as a teacher and as a parent even  participating in some of their  shenanigans too  there's character development happening  and i love the way this show reinvents  these characters and creates new arcs  for them  even raph donnie and mikey all have  little stories of growth along the way  like raph learning his place on the team  and trusting his brothers more  donny becoming more humble about his  intelligence and mikey having to be more  brave  they're still the characters we know and  love deep down below all the flash  we just get to see them evolving in new  ways that we didn't really get to see in  any of the previous series as the tmnt  are usually a bit more static in their  personalities  this isn't just booty shaking meme spout  and chaos  and while there's certainly an audience  for that and it isn't necessarily a bad  thing  i appreciate the hell out of ryze for  not falling into the pitfall of  just trying to emulate what's  financially successful right now  and outside of its deep characters and  fun tone it's got something going for it  that deserves all the praise it gets  and way more this show is by far  the most visually interesting tmnt  series  with its colorful and sharp character  designs its beautifully rendered  environments  and its emphasis on expressive fluid  high-energy movement  i think this is a beautiful blend of  western and eastern animation  because it also has some pretty strong  anime influence on its fight  choreography and some of its visual gags  i mentioned previously that the 2012  series felt limited by being a cgi  rendered show because  they couldn't model as many new  locations characters and environments as  a 2d show  and this one proves that right by having  zero limitations  the turtles feel like they can go  anywhere and  do anything and they do it with such  grace and style  i can't think of any animated show i've  seen that was this  ambitious with its visuals this is the  spider-verse of tv cartoons and i'm glad  nickelodeon never slashed this show's  absurdly high animation budget  some people chalk rise up to just being  pure eye candy with no substance but i  disagree  there's a lot going on that you have to  be paying attention to appreciate  underneath the slick visuals  i also can't get enough of this show's  sense of humor ah yes so in this case a  game of basketball  donny i know you know how to say it  we're all on to you  the dialogue doesn't quite have that  same stink of written by a 45 year old  white guy trying to sound like a  teenager  all the slang used by the characters  feels natural and appropriate  or ironic when necessary and i don't  find myself cringing at the more  contemporary gags and story ideas  there's a whole episode where the  turtles are trying to watch a skating  competition on their phones while  stopping a robbery and they're too bad  at multitasking to do both  not only is this a funny setup for a  fight scene but it feels pretty on brand  for today's teenagers while not coming  off as preachy about it  it's not the same phone bad video game  bad stuff you keep seeing in the modern  simpsons episodes  the green knight rises also goes out of  its way to create new and interesting  villains but make sure they still  feel like common tmnt foes a lot of  these guys seem so  natural that i was sure they'd be modern  takes on characters from previous  cartoons  but nope they're wholly original my  favorite is baron draxum  he has this slow redemption arc from a  demonic scientist who hates humans to an  eccentric guy who lives among them and  protects them  that's pretty special you don't see  shredder or krang pulling a 180 like  that  i'm just not sure who voices him because  all the behind the scenes footage shows  an empty seat in the recording booth and  as for shredder they spend two seasons  building him up as a genuine  threat and only giving a taste of his  power during his first two appearances  in the season finale you learn he's  actually pretty heavily inspired by  tengu shredder from the 2003 show once  he's gotten his mind back he's  everything you'd hoped for from shredder  i just wish we got to see more of them  your essence will soon be mined  and then i will be unstoppable  the show goes out of its way to really  take him seriously  and the episodes involving him tend to  either have the best action sequences by  far  or the most emotional and serious  moments of the show  i do have to mention things i don't like  about the show of course to keep it fair  but that's a pretty short list  i wish we had gotten to see this show's  take on casey jones since he's also one  of my absolute favorites in the turtle  mythos though it would be hard for them  to make him much different from the 2012  version  wait what which leads me to the next one  this is two shows in a row with  april as a high schooler and i kind of  miss her being an adult  more like a big sister to the turtles  rather than a friend of their own age  props for not making her kiss any of  them though that earns you a lot of  points in my book  and finally the backstory and history of  this hidden magic city filled with yokai  is sort of vague and unclear but a major  chunk of the characters cast  come from here and it would have been  nice to know more about it  like why humans drove the yokai  underground or how this society works or  who runs it  the only glimpses we see are typically  just leo and the boys messing around  with this magic skeleton guy who lives  down there  and occasionally seeing more about this  world through him or the demon spider  lady who runs a magic fight club in the  basement of her hotel  i was just curious about this stuff and  would have liked to know more about it  it left me wanting more which isn't a  bad thing but  it would have made the show a little  more satisfying that's the rub of pacing  out information with your show so  slowly you run the risk of leaving  questions unanswered if it gets abruptly  cancelled for stupid reasons  for whatever brainless reason almost  half of this show's episodes originally  aired at 9am on a saturday  can you remember ever being awake that  early when you were a kid  or like even as an adult i don't think a  lot of people would catch that  no one wants to get up early on saturday  that's the one day a week you're allowed  to sleep in as a reward for the previous  five days of eight to three or nine to  five boring garbage  well i mean when we all collectively  still went to school and work  even though it got better with season  two airing more in the evenings on  friday  the damage was still done it doesn't  help that the show is only really on  cable and it doesn't stream anywhere  my 11 year old sister is 13 now and she  doesn't watch basic cable  it's all youtube and streaming platforms  i don't even have cable at my house and  i just had to buy the entire season on  amazon to see this show officially and  support it  you couldn't have put the first season  on hulu or netflix or something  so nickelodeon pumped an insane amount  of money into this show's budget  but decided it was best to be airing  exclusively on a platform that young  people have effectively abandoned  and a lot of it was premiering at times  no one could see it what sense does that  make  gee that sure does sound familiar why  does this keep happening  i feel like as cable television recedes  and streaming grows  more and more kids shows are just going  to keep sharing this  fate if there's any target demographic  you have to really  chase after it's the younger kids  because if they don't have immediate  access to something  they won't go searching for it and will  just find something else  to watch rise of the tmnt was a show  worth watching  i know a lot of folks out there may have  been apprehensive because it's not what  they were used to  but all you had to do was give it a  chance to win you over with its good  qualities  and more than that rise is a show worth  saving  right now the only plans for any  continuation are a netflix based movie  coming out eventually  but that's not the seth rogen one that's  a different thing  nickelodeon has a habit of tying up  shows they cut short with a random movie  dumped on netflix because they don't  feel like showing it on the channel  well i say that's not gonna cut the  ninja robot here this show ended way too  soon and deserves a second chance look  unfortunately reviving a show like  spectacular spider-man or earth's  mightiest heroes is kind of a pipe dream  because those creators have all moved on  to new projects and are busy  while the brand has evolved and those  characters have new shows to fill out  the gap  not to mention all the licensing and  rights agreements you have to dance  around to revive those  but ninja turtles is owned exclusively  by viacom and they don't really have  that issue  and i'm willing to bet the show's  creators haven't had time to pack up  shop and move on just yet  they still have episodes storyboarded  and ready to go if they wanted to make  them  if there's any good time to push for  this show to get picked back  up it's now let your voice be heard make  a ton of noise on social media directed  at nick and viacom that you want this  show to come back  and be aired in a new way nick has  stated that they had plans to start  producing more content for netflix and  putting avatar back on there was a sign  of goodwill to get that rolling  this is exactly what they're looking for  this show should release on netflix and  get at least another three seasons  before netflix inevitably also cancels  it because they always do that and god  damn it i wanted to know what was gonna  happen on santa clarita diet  but there's still a shot for this one to  live on and get a second life if we just  push for it  i kind of don't know if they work but  start some petitions get a hashtag  trending  politely ask the right people  let's make a move hashtag save rise of  tmnt  and say no to meanpool this show is  definitely different but at the end of  the day it's still  understood the core themes of tmnt with  brotherhood  family and absurd adventures that aren't  afraid to be silly or insane  if you haven't watched it before it's  definitely worth your time  and if you have watched it there's no  doubt you probably want to see more  so let's not let this one end up like so  many others let's  save this show because we still love  being a turtle  i love it when having no plan comes  [Music]  together  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

